Connect turning effects to force geometry and moment origins (@physics-planar-moment: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../../engineering/mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/statics/Moments-couples-and-the-location-of-a-force.lingua#r_77XC5BZ6V561GD2THKBYWK6F3F") { r_0EFFA51CCA2E1922AFE1E99879
A point-particle model keeps a body's total force but cannot distinguish forces applied on different parallel lines. An extended rigid-body model also tracks their turning effect. Begin with a planar sketch: x points right, y up, and positive moment is counterclockwise when viewed from positive z, out of the page. These choices form a right-handed frame.

**Read:** Engineering Statics sections 4.1–4.3 and 4.5, with the planar exercises, and University Physics Volume 1 section 10.6. Use the mathematical vector, projection and coordinate preparation. The full three-dimensional cross-product and rotational-dynamics treatment follows later; here its out-of-plane component has an explicit geometric construction.

For a force applied at r=(x,y) relative to the chosen origin, split it into (Fx,0) and (0,Fy). The vertical part has signed moment x Fy; the horizontal part has signed moment −y Fx. Their sum is M=x Fy−y Fx, measured in N·m when the coordinates use metres and forces use newtons. Equivalently, its magnitude is force magnitude times the perpendicular distance to the line of action. The symbol r×F denotes the cross product; for these planar vectors it is (0,0,M). A dot product answers a different question and does not compute this turning effect.

For r=(2,1) m and F=(3,4) N, the moment is 8−3=5 N·m. The force has magnitude 5 N. A unit normal to its line is (4/5,−3/5), whose dot product with r is 1 m, so the perpendicular-distance calculation independently gives magnitude 5 N·m. Moving the force along its own line adds a displacement parallel to F and changes neither result. Moving it to another parallel line generally changes the moment.

For several forces, write their resultant R as their vector sum and their total moment M as the sum of the individual moments plus any applied couples. Move the origin by c=(cx,cy), keeping the axes fixed. Substituting r′=r−c gives M′=M−(cx Ry−cy Rx). Thus zero resultant force makes total moment independent of origin. This is a derived identity, not a rule that every single force has the same moment everywhere.

Two forces (0,−6) N at (0,0) m and (0,6) N at (2,0) m have zero resultant and a +12 N·m couple. No change of origin removes it. A body subject only to this couple cannot be in static equilibrium even though its net force vanishes. Moment and work have the same force-times-length dimensions, but moment is not an energy transfer: work requires displacement and its own physical account.

**Work:** reproduce the component and perpendicular-distance calculations. Shift the origin to (1,3) m for both examples. Check the couple's unchanged total and the single force's changed value. State which force and couple must be added when relocating a force to preserve a rigid body's external account. Reuse the [shared moments lesson](../../engineering/mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/statics/Moments-couples-and-the-location-of-a-force.lingua#r_77XC5BZ6V561GD2THKBYWK6F3F)'s original example next; study its planar account now and retain its broader chapter-4 assignment for later three-dimensional formation.

**Reading companions:** [Engineering Statics — Baker and Haynes](../../engineering/mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/sources/Engineering-Statics-Baker-and-Haynes.lingua#r_3DXGKGQSPPA9YDNH2WSB8XRN80); [University Physics Volume 1 — OpenStax](../fluid-dynamics/sources/University-Physics-Volume-1.lingua#r_2W7QCEHZ45XDB3AWVG9NTV36F6). Use the selected passages named above; their broader assignments retain later preparation.

**Shared mathematical preparation:** [projections](../../mathematics/core/Resolve-a-vector-into-a-projection-and-an-orthogonal-remainder.lingua#r_0E1019900E901787E7D6D8001B) and [angles and trigonometry](../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/foundations/Angles-and-trigonometry.lingua#r_0FPTH1YN87PZ97SHWGX19ZJZDZ).

